# Phase 4: Python Preprocessing & Distributed Scalability

Complete the final phase: fix preprocessing bottlenecks via multiprocessing, add distributed shard assignment, and deliver exhaustive benchmarking with visual plots.

## Proposed Changes

### 1. Streaming Multiprocessing Pipeline

#### [MODIFY] [pipeline.py](file:///c:/Users/PC/Desktop/vlm-image-loader/preprocessing/pipeline.py)

The current pipeline has two critical bottlenecks:
- **Sequential processing**: `_run_local_shuffle_pipeline` and the main path both iterate samples one-at-a-time on a single thread
- **Full-dataset buffering**: `_run_local_shuffle_pipeline` collects ALL preprocessed samples in `preprocessed: list[dict]` before writing — this blows up RAM on large datasets

**Changes:**
- Add `_init_worker_tokenizer()` / `_process_sample_in_worker()` as module-level functions for `ProcessPoolExecutor` (tokenizer must be initialized per-worker since HuggingFace tokenizers aren't fork-safe)
- New `_run_streaming_pipeline()` that accumulates samples shard-by-shard up to `max_samples_per_shard`, processes the chunk in parallel via `ProcessPoolExecutor`, writes the shard, and flushes memory before the next chunk
- `run_pipeline()` dispatches to the streaming path when `num_workers >= 1`, falls back to the existing sequential path for `num_workers == 0`
- The streaming path respects `config.shuffling` (none / global / local) — global shuffle shuffles the full sample list before chunking; local shuffle shuffles each chunk before writing

